5. Structures, unions, enumerations, and typedefs:

    C lets you create your own types.

        6.a. Structures (struct)

            Groups related values that may have different types.

                struct Student
                {
                    int     age;
                    chat    grade;
                    float   average;
                }

                struct Student  s;

                s  = {20, 'A', 91.5f};

            TRhe variable s contains an age, grade, and the average.

        6.b. Unions (union)

            Allows different members to share the same memory region.

                union Data
                {
            	    int     number;
                    float   decimal;
                    char    letter;
                }

                union Data      d;

                d.number = 10;

        6.b. Enumerations (enum)

            Defines Named integer constants.

                enum Day
                {
                	MONDAY;
                    TUESDAY;
                    WEDNESDAY;
                }

                enum Day today = TUESDAY;

            By default, the values are 0, 1, 2, respectively.

        6.4. Type alias (typedef)

            Gives an existing type another name.

                typedef unsigned int uint;

                uint age = 20;

            typedef does not create a new fundamental type; it creates an alias.
